10:32:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Not everyone in the 1990s thought the Internet would fail. By the mid-to-late 1990s, many businesses, researchers, investors, and ordinary users were extremely enthusiastic about it. The idea that “everyone dismissed it as a fad” is mostly a retrospective myth built from a handful of famously wrong predictions. But skepticism was understandable for several reasons: - **Early Internet was awkward and slow.** Dial-up connections, confusing software, unreliable websites, and long download times made it hard to imagine it becoming an everyday utility. - **There was no proven business model yet.** Many early online companies lost money, online advertising was untested, and people were uncertain whether customers would trust online payments or buy products without seeing them first. - **Existing industries looked too strong.** Newspapers, TV, retailers, telecoms, and banks had established distribution systems. Some leaders assumed the Internet would remain a niche supplement rather than replace parts of those systems. - **The Web was initially associated with hobbyists and academics.** Before mass-market services, it could look like a technical playground rather than a platform for commerce, entertainment, communication, and public life. - **Hype invited backlash.** As Internet enthusiasm became excessive—especially during the dot-com boom—skeptics correctly noticed that many companies had absurd valuations and weak fundamentals. They sometimes confused “many dot-com businesses will fail” with “the Internet itself will fail.” - **Past technology fads made caution reasonable.** People had seen heavily promoted technologies fail to meet expectations, such as interactive television, certain early online services, and various consumer-electronics trends. The key distinction is that critics were often right that **specific companies and business plans** would collapse. And many did during the 2000–2002 dot-com crash. But the underlying network, software, and cultural shift kept growing. So the 1990s were not a simple story of everyone rejecting the Internet. They were a mix of genuine technological uncertainty, irrational speculation, practical skepticism, and very real belief that it would transform society.
