10:33:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—it counts as evidence of what *your parents and teachers experienced and believed*, especially in their community or profession. A lot of people really did dismiss it or treat it as a temporary novelty. In the early 1990s, that was particularly plausible: home access was uncommon, dial-up was frustrating, and the Internet seemed like a weird place for academics, computer enthusiasts, and chat rooms. Even later in the decade, many adults saw it as useful for email or research but doubted that it would become central to shopping, media, work, dating, banking, and social life. But personal memory can make a view seem more universal than it was. People tend to remember the skeptics—especially people who were confidently wrong—more vividly than the many people who were enthusiastic, building websites, buying stocks, starting companies, or adopting email. There were also two different claims often blended together: 1. **“This Internet thing won’t last.”** Some people genuinely said this. 2. **“The dot-com boom is irrational and many Internet businesses will crash.”** This was also common—and largely correct. The crash made skeptics feel vindicated, even though Internet use and infrastructure continued expanding. So your parents and teachers are not necessarily misremembering or exaggerating their own world. Their experience is real. It just doesn’t establish that *nobody* thought the Internet would last; plenty of people clearly did, and invested careers, money, and institutions accordingly.
