10:33:21 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
10:33:21 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=872
10:33:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fair conclusion if the material you’ve read is concentrated in the early-to-mid 1990s, or in mainstream publications aimed at people outside tech. Skepticism was absolutely widespread, and many respected commentators underestimated how quickly the Internet would become normal. The important qualification is that it was not a stable, universal consensus across the entire decade. - **Around 1990–1993:** It was very easy to see the Internet as a specialist network: slow, text-heavy, difficult to access, and mostly used by universities, government, and technical people. Doubting mass adoption was a mainstream position. - **Around 1994–1996:** The Web, browsers, consumer ISPs, and services such as email changed the picture quickly. Optimism grew rapidly, but many people still thought it was overhyped or commercially unimportant. - **Around 1997–1999:** The dominant public problem was often the reverse: enormous optimism, including obviously excessive dot-com investment. At that point, many people expected the Internet to reshape business and culture—even if they disagreed about which companies would succeed. Old skeptical quotes are also unusually memorable and widely recopied because they make great hindsight stories: “Look how wrong this expert was.” A cautious or enthusiastic prediction usually is not preserved in the same way. And sometimes quotes are stripped of scope—for example, someone may have doubted that people would buy groceries online, or that a particular online service would work, rather than claiming that networked computing itself would disappear. Still, your broader point stands: the Internet’s eventual scale was not obvious to many educated, informed people. The fact that it now seems inevitable is partly hindsight. In the early years, the skeptical case was not absurd; it was based on real limitations and on the lack of a clear path from clunky dial-up access to the global infrastructure we have now.
