10:33:42 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
10:33:42 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=1311
10:33:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It does not have to be either “rare flukes” or “everyone believed it.” A more accurate statement is: **Thinking the Internet was a fad, overhyped, or unlikely to become central to daily life was a common and respectable view for much of the early and mid-1990s.** It was not an eccentric position. Those quotes and articles matter because they show real mainstream skepticism—from journalists, executives, academics, and commentators—not merely your family’s experience. The Internet was slow, expensive, unfamiliar, difficult to use, and initially lacked many of the services that later made it indispensable. Given that context, dismissal was understandable. But it still was not a unanimous consensus, especially by the late 1990s. At the same time skeptics were writing those articles: - companies were building Web businesses and Internet infrastructure; - consumer Internet use was rising quickly; - major media organizations were launching websites; - investors were pouring money into dot-coms; - many people were making very expansive predictions about online commerce and communication. In fact, by roughly 1997–1999, public opinion in business and finance often swung from skepticism to **excessive belief**. The dot-com bubble was evidence that many people did think the Internet would be huge—though they were often wrong about which firms would profit. So the fair historical version is not “nobody believed in it,” nor “skepticism was just a few embarrassing quotations.” It is: > Early Internet skepticism was widespread and often mainstream; enthusiasm was also substantial and became dominant in many influential circles later in the decade. The famous dismissive quotes are memorable partly because they turned out wrong, but they were not invented anomalies. They reflect a real, common attitude in an era when the Internet’s eventual importance was far from obvious.
