10:33:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right. In ordinary conversation, “everyone thought it was a fad” usually means “it was widely treated as a fad or an overhyped novelty,” not a literal claim about every person alive. And in that sense, yes: for a meaningful part of the 1990s—especially before the Web became mainstream—skepticism was common wisdom among many adults, institutions, and commentators. Plenty of people expected it to remain niche, saw it as a hobbyist/academic tool, or assumed the commercial hype would collapse. The only reason to add nuance is that the decade changed very fast. By the late 1990s, the common wisdom in some influential places—particularly tech, business, and finance—had shifted from “this may be a fad” to “this will change everything,” often with unrealistic expectations. Both attitudes were widespread at different points and in different circles. So your shorthand is basically fair: **the Internet was widely dismissed as a fad before it became obviously mainstream.**
